## 1. Introduction
Lung cancer has the highest mortality rate among cancers worldwide [1]. Clinical anticancer drugs have many drawbacks, such as poor bioavailability, low specificity, resistance induction, short circulation life, and low solubility, resulting in huge damage to normal cells during treatment [2–4]. Thus, there is an urgent need for developing new generations of antitumor weapons for cancer therapy.
Gene therapy has emerged as a powerful tool for treating challenging diseases, such as cancer [5,6]. Suicide therapy uses tumorspecifically expressed drug-activated enzymes (suicide genes, such as herpes simplex virus type I thymidine kinase, abbreviated as HSVtk) to complete systemic delivery and avoid toxicity in normal cells [7], and thus can be considered as an effective therapeutic biological bomb. HSVtk catalyzes the phosphorylation of the cytotoxic nucleoside analog ganciclovir (GCV), which can be incorporated into the DNA of actively proliferating cells, leading to the disruption of DNA replication and the halt of cell division [8]. Interestingly, GCV is a poor substrate for mammalian thymidine kinase but is sensitive to HSVtk. The toxic effects are mostly seen in cancer cells, which proliferate rapidly [9].
Furthermore, HSVtk can generate a ‘‘bystander effect”, through which neighboring cancer cells suffer from toxicity, although the cells do not take up HSVtk directly [10]. To increase the specific activation in lung cancer cells, it is feasible to use carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) as a tumor-specific promoter to drive the biological bomb [11]. However, the use of biological bombs for cancer therapy have encountered some difficulties, such as the enzymatic degradation property, large size, or strong negative charges, which impede successful entry into the cells [12]. Conventional vehicles, such as liposomes [5], cationic polymers [13], and inorganic nanoparticles [14], are good candidates for gene delivery. However, the instability and strongly positive charge limit their applications. Furthermore, due to the disadvantages of the present delivery systems, pHSVtk and GCV were administered separately, where pHSVtk was usually administered first, followed by GCV injection, causing inconvenience for the utilization of biological bombs and increasing the potential side effects for nonspecific delivery.
Recently, functional metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have been used as vehicles for drug delivery [15,16]. The zeolitic imidazolate framework possesses a high surface area, tailorable/or flexible pore size, and satisfactory degradability [17]. Our previous work indicated that ZIF-8 showed an extremely high binding affinity with nucleic acids [18]. However, the use of ZIF-8 as a platform for the codelivery of nucleic acids and small molecule compounds has not yet been reported. The surface properties of ZIF-8-based nanoparticles should also be considered because in vivo use often takes place in complicated physiological environments, which can affect their stability, circulation lifetime, and immune clearance.
Cell membrane coating mimics the surface properties of the source cells and presents multiple functions for drug delivery [19]. Previous work indicated that the cell membranes derived from cancer cells [18], erythrocytes [20], platelets [21], neutrophils [22], stem cells [23], and bacteria [24] were used to camouflage the nanoparticles, which enhanced the circulation lifetime, stability, and cell-like functions [25,26]. Camouflage with cell membranes derived from human bone marrow mesenchymal stem cells (hBMSCs) has shown great potential in cancer therapy [27]. They possess innate tumor-targeting abilities due to targeting ligands and surface antigenic diversity, such as adhesion ligands (Pselectin glycoprotein ligand-1 and sialyl LewisX) and chemokine receptors (CCR2, CXCL12, and CXCR4) [28,29]. Furthermore, BMSCs show low immunogenicity. Previous work indicated that BMSC membrane-coated nanogels achieved targeted delivery of doxorubicin (DOX) to HeLa cells both in vitro and in vivo [30]. However, their use in lung cancer therapy has not yet been reported.
Herein, we report a straightforward strategy to deliver the biological bomb. Briefly, the zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8) was used to carry ganciclovir (GCV) and herpes simplex virus type I thymidine kinase plasmids (pHSVtk) (FORE) (Fig. 1A). After facile modification with cell membranes derived from human bone marrow mesenchymal stem cells (hBMSCs), biological bomb-loaded biomimetic nanoparticles BEFORE (hBMSC membrane/framework-8/ganciclovir (GCV)/herpes simplex virus type I thymidine kinase plasmids) were formed. We analyzed the physicochemical properties of the biomimetic nanoparticles and evaluated their performance in lung cancer inhibition both in vitro and in vivo (Fig. 1B). We predict that this style of biological bomb will be able to inhibit lung cancer effectively based on the biomimetic structure.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
Zinc nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , $98\%$ and 2- methylimidazole $(99\%)$ were purchased from Sigma–Aldrich (USA). The HSVtk plasmids were constructed by cloning the pHSVtk gene into the pcDNA3.1 vector, with the cytomegalovirus promoter replaced by a CEA promoter (GeneBiogist Technology Company, China). Ganciclovir (GCV) was purchased from Macklin Reagent Company (Shanghai, China). 1,1-Dioctadecyl-3,3,3,3-tetra methylindotricarbo cyanine iodide (DiR), $^{1,1^{\prime}}$ -dioctadecyl-3,3,30,3 0-tetra methylindodicarbocyanine 4-chlorobenzenesulfonate salt (DiD), Live/Dead Cell Staining Kit, LysoTracker Red and 40,6-diami dino-2-phenylindole (DAPI) were purchased from Thermo Fisher Scientific (USA). Fetal bovine serum (FBS), penicillin, and streptomycin were purchased from Gibco (USA). Male BALB/c-nu mice (6 weeks old) were purchased from Beijing Vital River Laboratory Animal Technology Co., Ltd. (Beijing, China).
## 2.2. Instruments
Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images were obtained by a JEM-2100Plus (200 kV, JEOL, Japan). The UV–vis absorbance was measured by UV-1800 spectroscopy (Shimadzu, Japan). The particle size and zeta potential were measured by a Nano ZS (Malvern, UK). Fluorescence microscopy images were observed using a confocal laser scanning microscope (CLSM, LSM880, Zeiss, Germany) and an inverted fluorescence microscope (IX73, Olympus, Japan).
2.3. Synthesis of FORE (Zeolitic imidazolate framework-8/ganciclovir/ herpes simplex virus type I thymidine kinase plasmid)
pHSVtk $(1~\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mL})$ and GCV $(50\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mL})$ were dissolved in a solution of 2-methylimidazole $\mathbf{\chi}^{\prime}100\ \mathbf{\mu}\mathbf{g}/\mathbf{m}\mathbf{L})$ in water and stirred at room temperature for 10 min. $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O(5\upmu g/m L)}$ was added under stirring. FORE was separated from the dispersion by centrifugation $(14000\mathrm{rpm})$ , washed with deionized water three times, resuspended in sterile PBS and stored at $4~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . ZIF-8/pHSVtk and ZIF-8/GCV were synthesized with the same method.
## 2.4. Extraction of hBMSCs cell membranes
Human bone mesenchymal stem cells (hBMSCs) were cultured in $10\ \mathrm{cm}$ dishes to reach $80\%$ confluence. Cell membranes were extracted by a membrane and cytosol protein extraction kit (Beyotime, China), and the concentration was measured using the bicinchoninic acid (BCA) method. The samples were stored at $-80^{\circ}\mathsf C$ until use.
2.5. Preparation of BEFORE (bone mesenchymal stem cell membranes/ zeolitic imidazolate framework-8/ganciclovir/herpes simplex virus type I thymidine kinase plasmid)
The prepared FORE was camouflaged with cell membranes extracted from hBMSCs (BM) at $0.5/1,\ 1/1,\ 2/1,\ 5/1$ , and $10/1$ (weight of BM/weight of FORE) and treated with ultrasound for 5 min. BEFORE was obtained by extrusion 11 times using a Liposofast-Basic Extrusion Machine (Avestin Inc., Canada).
## 2.6. Characterization of the nanoparticles
The particle size, zeta potential, and polydispersity index (PDI) of ZIF-8 nanoparticles, FORE, and BEFORE were measured by a Nano ZS (Malvern, UK) at room temperature. The samples were prediluted by deionized water $(3{:}7,\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v})$ to ensure surface charges at low ion intensity.
TEM images were obtained by a JEM-2100Plus $200~\mathrm{kV}$ , JEOL, Japan). Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) analysis was performed in an ASAP 2020 Plus analyzer (Micromeritics Instrument Corporation, USA). X-ray diffraction (XRD) analysis was carried out by an X-ray diffractometer (Ultima IV, Rigaku, Japan).
## 2.7. Drug loading efficiency and loading content
FORE was prepared by the ‘‘one-pot” method, and the dispersion was centrifuged $(14000~\mathrm{rpm})$ for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ at $4~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ The supernatant was collected, and agarose gel electrophoresis was used to determine the loading efficiency of the plasmids. UV–vis spectroscopy was used to obtain the loading efficiency of GCV. FORE was prepared by the ‘‘one-pot” method, and the dispersion was centrifuged $(14000~\mathrm{rpm})$ for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ at $4^{\circ}C$ The supernatant was collected, and agarose gel electrophoresis was used to determine the loading efficiency of the plasmids. UV–vis spectroscopy was used to obtain the loading efficiency of GCV. The drug loading efficiency was calculated according to the following equation:
Drug loading efficiency (LE) $\left(\%\right)=$ (weight of total drug-weight of free drug)/(weight of total drug) $\times100$ .
To quantify the drug loading contents, FORE and BEFORE were freeze-dried and added to a pH of 5.0 until the nanoparticles were degraded completely. The loading contents were detected according to the following equation:
Drug loading content (LC) $\left(\%\right)=$ (Weight of loaded drugs/Weight of nanoparticles) $\times100$ .
## 2.8. In vitro drug release
pHSVtk was labeled with a Label IT Cy5 Labeling Kit (Mirus Bio, USA) according to the manufacturer’s instructions. Cy5-pHSVtk was used to study the drug release behavior of ZIF-8. ZIF-8/Cy5- pHSVtk was synthesized and placed into a dialysis bag (molecular weight cutoff, $3500~\mathrm{{Da}}.$ ). Two dialysis bags containing $1~\mathrm{mL}$ solution were prepared and submerged in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ PBS $\mathrm{\langlepH}=5.0$ and 7.4) separately. After incubation for 0, 1, 2, 4, 6, 8, 12, 24, 36, 48, 72, and $96\mathrm{~h~}$ on a shaker at room temperature, $1~\mathrm{mL}$ aliquots were withdrawn and replenished with fresh PBS. The fluorescence was detected using an $\mathbf{M}1000$ multifunctional microplate reader (Tecan, Switzerland) to determine the content of Cy5-pHSVtk.
## 2.9. Cell culture
NCI-H1299 and human pulmonary alveolar epithelial cells (HPAEpiC) were obtained from ATCC (Manassas, USA) and cultured in Dulbecco’s modified Eagle’s medium (DMEM, Gibco Life Technologies, USA) with $10\%$ inactivated fetal bovine serum, $100~\mathrm{U}/\$ mL penicillin, and $100\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ streptomycin at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in a humidified atmosphere with $5\%\thinspace{\mathrm{CO}}_{2}$ .
## 2.10. In vitro cellular uptake
NCI-H1299 cells were seeded in confocal dishes at a density of $1\times10^{5}$ cells per well to reach $70\%$ confluence. The cells were cultured with nanoparticles of different plasmid concentrations (0.5, 1, 1.5, 2, $2.5~\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mL})$ in DMEM for $^{9\mathrm{~h~}}$ and then washed with PBS and stained with DAPI and FITC-phalloidin. Confocal laser scanning microscopy (CLSM, LSM880, Zeiss, Germany) was applied to observe the cellular uptake of nanoparticles. The cellular uptake of nanoparticles over time (1, 3, 6, 9, and $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ ) was investigated using the abovementioned method.
To quantitatively analyze the cellular uptake, flow cytometry (FACS) analysis was performed. NCI-H1299 cells were seeded in 12-well plates with $1~\mathrm{mL}$ DMEM, cultured at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in a humidified atmosphere with $5\%\thinspace{\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , and then treated the same way as in CLSM observation. The cells were digested with $0.25\%$ trypsin and resuspended in $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ PBS. ImageStreamX flow cytometry (Amnis Company, USA) was utilized to analyze the Cy5-positive cells.
## 2.11. In vitro cytotoxicity assays
The Live/Dead Cell Staining Kit was applied to determine the toxicity of nanoparticles in vitro. NCI-H1299 cells were seeded in confocal dishes at a density of $1\times10^{5}$ cells per well and incubated for $24\mathrm{h}$ . BEFO, BEFOR, or BEFOE were then incubated with the cells for $^{9\mathrm{~h~}}$ , and PBS-treated cells were used as the control. The transfection medium was removed and replaced with freshly prepared DMEM complete medium. The cells were incubated in the normal cell culture environment for another $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ , followed by staining with calcein-AM/PI for CLSM observations.
## 2.12. Bystander effect
BEFORE was used to transfect NCI-H1299 cells. The transfected cells were digested and resuspended in DMEM. Then, the suspension was mixed with normal NCI-H1299 cells at ratios of $10\%$ $20\%$ , $50\%$ , and $80\%$ and cultured in 96-well plates. After incubating for $24\mathrm{h}$ , a Cell Counting Kit-8 (CCK-8) (Dojindo, Japan) was applied to examine the viability.
## 2.13. Lysosome escape assay
Lysosome escape was performed to evaluate the release of pHSVtk within the cells. NCI-H1299 cells were seeded in confocal dishes at a density of $1\times10^{5}$ cells and incubated for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The cells were transfected by adding BM/ZIF-8/GCV/Cy5-pHSVtk (pHSVtk, $2\ \upmu\mathrm{g})$ . After incubating for the designated time intervals $^{\mathrm{~\textcent~}1\mathrm{~h~}}$ , ${\mathfrak{g}}{\mathfrak{h}}_{.}^{.}$ ), the cells were washed with PBS twice and stained with LysoTracker Green DND-22 (ThermoFisher Scientific, USA) for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ . CLSM was utilized to observe the distribution of nanoparticles in cells at different periods.
## 2.14. Western blot
NCI-H1299 cells were seeded in 6-well plates and cultured at $37^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in a humidified atmosphere with $5\%\thinspace{\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The medium was then removed and replaced by DMEM containing PBS, BEFO (BM/ZIF-8), BEFOR (BM/ZIF-8/GCV), BEFOE (BM/ZIF-8/ pHSVtk), FORE (ZIF-8/GCV/pHSVtk), or BEFORE (BM/ZIF-8/GCV/ pHSVtk). The transfection medium was removed and replaced with freshly prepared DMEM complete medium. After incubation for another $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ , fresh RIPA buffer with complete protease inhibitor was added, and proteins were extracted. The concentration was measured by the BCA method and then applied for sodium dodecyl sulfate–polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (SDS-PAGE) analysis, transfer, and exposure.
## 2.15. Animal experiments
Male BALB/c-nu mice (6 weeks old) were raised in a specific pathogen-free environment. The animal experiments followed the Institutional Authority for Laboratory Animal Care of Guangzhou Medical University (GY2020-053).
## 2.16. Biodistribution and circulation lifetime in vivo
The tumor model was established by subcutaneously injecting NCI-H1299 cells into the matrix gel. Cy5-pHSVtk, ZIF-8/GCV/Cy5- pHSVtk, or BM/ZIF-8/GCV/Cy5-pHSVtk were synthesized and intravenously injected into tumor-bearing mice. At predetermined intervals of 1, 4, 8, 12, 24, and $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the mice were anesthetized with $2\%$ isoflurane and then imaged by a small animal imaging system (CRi Maestro, USA). The fluorescence analysis was processed by Maestro V3.0.A.
We also evaluated the circulation lifetime in vivo. Tumorbearing BALB/c-nu mice (6 weeks old) were randomly divided into 3 groups. Each group was intravenously injected with Cy5-pHSVtk, ZIF-8/GCV/Cy5-pHSVtk, or BM/ZIF-8/GCV/Cy5-pHSVtk $\left(\mathfrak{n}\ \mathfrak{s}\right)$ . Blood samples were taken 1, 4, 8, 12, 24, and $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ after drug administration. The fluorescence was detected with an M1000 multifunctional microplate reader.
## 2.17. In vivo anticancer study
The tumor model was established by subcutaneously injecting NCI-H1299 cells into the matrix gel. Tumor-bearing mice were obtained after 2 weeks and randomly divided into 6 groups (saline, BEFO, BEFOR, BEFOE, FORE, or BEFORE). The formulations were administered through an intravenous tail injection every 3 days. The tumor volumes were monitored every day. After being treated 8 times, the mice were sacrificed. The main organs of the heart, liver, spleen, lung, kidney, and tumors were collected and fixed to evaluate the acute toxicity of therapeutic agents.
The volume of tumors was calculated according to the following equation:
V $(\mathrm{mm}^{3})=0.5\mathrm{ab}^{2}$ .
where ‘‘a” stands for maximum tumor diameter and ‘‘b” stands for minimum tumor diameter.
## 2.18. In situ apoptosis assay
The tumors were fixed with $4\%$ paraformaldehyde and embedded in paraffin. Paraffin-embedded tissue sections $(5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}})$ were used for TdT-mediated dUTP nick-end labeling (TUNEL) analysis. Apoptosis in different groups was detected by an in situ cell death detection kit (Roche, USA).
## 2.19. Hematoxylin-eosin (HE) staining
The major organs, such as the heart, liver, spleen, lung, and kidney obtained from the above mice, were fixed in $4\%$ paraformaldehyde at room temperature, multigradient dehydrated with ethanol, paraffin-embedded, sectioned, and stained with HE. An inverted fluorescence microscope (IX73, Olympus, Japan) was applied to observe the biopsy and analyze the histological characteristics.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of BEFORE
We prepared biological bomb-loaded ZIF-8 nanostructures according to the one-pot synthesis method by mixing methylimidazole and zinc acetate. A mixture of methylimidazole and pHSVtk/GCV was used as a precursor, and zinc ions were used as an additive to obtain gene and small molecule compound-loaded ZIF-8 nanostructures (FORE). We used a nitrogen adsorption surface area analyzer to characterize the surface area of the nanoparticles. The surface areas for ZIF-8, ZIF-8/GCV, and ZIF-8/pHSVtk were $1269~\mathrm{\m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},$ $1176~\mathrm{~m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},$ and $1041~\mathrm{\m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},$ respectively (Fig. S1A), confirming the effective loading of the genes. In addition, the surface area decreased to $1025~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ after the encapsulation of both pHSVtk and GCV (Fig. S1A). We further characterized the crystalline ZIF-8-based nanostructures by $\mathsf{X}$ -ray diffraction (XRD). Compared with ZIF-8, ZIF-8/GCV, and ZIF-8/pHSVtk, the diffraction peaks of FORE were decreased significantly, implying that the loading of both drugs changed the crystalline ZIF-8- based nanoparticles (Fig. S1B). Agarose gel electrophoresis indicated that ZIF-8 showed a high loading efficiency (LE) to pHSVtk at a weight ratio of 200 to 1, with an LE of $85.2\%$ (Fig. S2A). Meanwhile, the LE for GCV was $\sim23.2\%$ . The relatively high LE of pHSVtk and GCV may be beneficial in improving transfection and cell inhibition (Fig. S2B). Previous work indicated that nucleic acids are encapsulated in the pores of the MOF structure [31,32]. In addition, GCV possesses many kinds of functional groups, such as amino and hydroxyl groups, that can be utilized to form covalent bonds with inorganic metal clusters $(Z\mathsf{n}^{2+})$ [33,34]. GCV can also be encapsulated in the pores due to the high porosity [34]. Furthermore, ZIF-8 showed the capability to prevent pHSVtk plasmids from nuclease degradation, in which $\sim70\%$ of the plasmids were preserved after incubation with the serum (Fig. S3A). In contrast, free pHSVtk was nearly degraded within $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ (Fig. S3B). The protection of nucleic acids by ZIF-8 ensured the resulting biological effects in the complex physiological environment.
TEM analysis indicated that ZIF-8 and FORE were hexagonal (Fig. 2A). FORE possessed a pH-responsive property and significantly degraded under acidic conditions compared with neutral conditions (Fig. S4A and S4B). Dynamic light scanning (DLS) analysis suggested that the size of ZIF-8 or FORE was $148.0\pm2.3~\mathrm{nm}$ or $154.0\pm3.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 2A), with zeta potentials of $40.9\pm4.2~\mathrm{mV}$ or $-25.4\pm3.8~\mathrm{mV}$ , respectively (Fig. 2B). The narrow size distribution in the water demonstrated the excellent dispersibility of particles. ZIF-8-based nanoparticles were prepared at different molar ratios of 2-methylimidazole to $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ to optimize the conditions (Fig. S5). The optimized molar ratio of 2-methylimidazole to $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ was 72.5, with a size of $140\pm4~\mathrm{{nm}}$ (Fig. S5A), and zeta potential of $37.75\pm0.49~\mathrm{mV}$ (Fig. S5B). The stability of the nanoparticles is essential for the therapeutic effect. DLS analysis indicated that BEFORE showed better stability than FORE with a significant increase in size (Fig. S6). hBMSCs were expanded, and surface markers, such as CD29, CD44, CD73, CD105, and CD166, were identified to be positive, while CD31, CD34, and CD45 were negative (Fig. S7), which was consistent with our previous work [35]. We used the cell membranes derived from hBMSCM (BM) to camouflage FORE (BEFORE). The ratios of BM to FORE were screened by considering the size, polydispersity index (PDI), and zeta potential. We found that BM to FORE at a weight ratio of 2 showed optimized parameters (Table S1), and possessed a size of $198.3\pm2.1~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , PDI of $0.32\pm0.08$ , and zeta potential of $-23.0\pm1$ . $7~\mathrm{mV}$ . TEM analysis showed that BEFORE possessed an apparent core–shell structure (Fig. 2A). The distribution of BEFORE was uniform without detectable aggregation, indicating good stability (Fig. 2A). The negatively charged delivery system can avoid excessive particle-cell interactions to reduce toxicity because the surface of mammalian cells is usually negatively charged (Fig. 2B). To investigate the release property from FORE, the cumulative release curves indicated that the release efficiency of pHSVtk and GCV was much higher at $\mathsf{p H}~5.0$ than at $\mathsf{p H}7.4$ , implying that the ZIF-8- based nanoparticles were sensitive to the acidic environment (Fig. 2C). More than $80\%$ of both components were released within $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $\mathsf{p H}5.0$ , but $<50\%$ were released at $\mathsf{p H}7.4$ (Fig. 2C). The pH responsiveness facilitates drug release under acidic physiological environments of the tumor, which is a desirable property for drug delivery. We also tested the loading content (LC) of both FORE and BEFORE. The LC of FORE was $0.49\pm0.01\%$ and $7.54\pm0.77\%$ for pHSVtk and GCV, respectively, and that of BEFORE was $0.13\pm0.02\%$ and $1.90\pm0.20\%$ for pHSVtk and GCV, respectively (Table S2). The SDS-PAGE analysis indicated that BEFORE retained most of the proteins on BM (Fig. 2D). The process of membrane camouflage does not affect the protein composition of membranes. The biomimetic MOF possesses a uniform size, high loading efficiency, and pH-responsiveness, making it a powerful drug delivery system.
## 3.2. Optimization of the transfection conditions
To optimize the transfection conditions of the functionalized delivery system, we characterized dose- and time-dependence. For good observation, we used Cy5 to label pHSVtk. When the concentration of pHSVtk increased to $2~\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mL}$ , over $90\%$ of the cells were Cy5-positive, implying effective cellular uptake (Fig. 3A). When the concentration of pHSVtk was up to $2.5~\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mL}$ , the cellular uptake did not change significantly (Fig. 3A). During different observation periods (1, 3, 6, 9, and $12\mathrm{h}$ ), there was remarkable cellular uptake at $^{9\mathrm{~h~}}$ in NCI-H1299 cells (Fig. 3B), with Cy5-positive cells over $90\%$ . The results indicated that the optimal dose and incubation time were $2\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mL}$ and $^{9\mathrm{h}}$ , respectively, so we selected these parameters for the following in vitro and in vivo experiments. To study the endocytosis mechanism of BEFORE by NCI-H1299 cells (Fig. S8), NCI-H1299 cells were treated with various endocytosis inhibitors. The cellular uptake of BEFORE was obviously reduced in the presence of chlorpromazine (CPZ, an inhibitor of clathrin-dependent endocytosis), whereas exposure to filimarisin (FMR, an inhibitor of caveolin-dependent endocytosis) and amiloride (AMO, an inhibitor of macropinocytosis) showed slightly decreased effects, indicating that BEFORE was internalized primarily by clathrin-dependent endocytosis, generally referred to as ‘‘receptor-mediated endocytosis”, in collaboration with caveolindependent endocytosis and macropinocytosis. To explore the specificity of cellular uptake, we used three different cell lines to analyze BEFORE uptake by CLSM (Fig. S9). Compared to the NCIH1299 cells, the uptake of BEFORE by the MEL-12 and NIH-3 T3 cells was obviously decreased, indicating that the BM-modified FORE nanoparticles can improve specific uptake by NCI-H1299.
## 3.3. Cellular uptake of pHSVtk-containing nanoparticles
We also examined the cellular uptake induced by different formulations. Compared with the FORE, BEFORE promoted cellular uptake, which implied that camouflage with BM improved the surface function of nanoparticles, resulting in the enhancement of cellular uptake by lung cancer cells (Fig. 4A). This property could help stimulate faster drug absorption during treatment.
Successful lysosomal escape is a prerequisite for gene therapy [26]. To explore lysosomal escape, NCI-H1299 cells were incubated with BEFORE, followed by observation of the morphological variation of the lysosomes with CLSM. BEFORE gradually entered the cytoplasm and colocalized with the lysosome (marked by red arrows) after $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ of incubation (Fig. 4B). After incubation for $^{9\mathrm{~h~}}$ the overlay fluorescence was significantly reduced, implying efficient lysosomal escape (Fig. 4B).
The camouflage with cell membranes derived from hBMSCs increased the cellular uptake by lung cancer cells. In addition, ZIF-8 showed the capability of improving lysosome escape. These properties ensured effective antitumor therapy.
## 3.4. Tumor cell inhibition in vitro
We performed a series of studies to evaluate the gene transfection and toxicity of the intracellular delivery system. Western blot analysis indicated that BEFORE could efficiently transfect pHSVtk into NCI-H1299 cells (Fig. 5A and 5B). The bystander effect is an attractive property to kill neighboring cancer cells induced by HSVtk. We incubated transfected NCI-H1299 cells with untransfected cells and found that the transfection efficiency of pHSVtk was close to the cell viability. When the transfected cells were $10\%$ , the cell viability induced by BEFORE was approximately $86.2\pm7.7\%$ . With the transfected cells reaching $50\%$ , the cell viability was approximately $24.7\ \pm\ 3.8\%$ . The data implied that the untransfected NCI-H1299 cells were killed due to toxic effects (Fig. 5C). We also investigated the cell inhibition induced by different formulations. First, we evaluated the viability of cells treated with BEFO, BEFOR, and BEFOE and found that these formulations showed low cytotoxicity (greater than $80\%$ ) at a working concentration of $800~\mathrm{\upmug/mL}$ (Fig. S10). The single treatment with pHSVtk or GCV-containing formulations also did not affect the cell viability significantly (Fig. S10). We further evaluated the treatment with the formulations, including BEFO, BEFOR, BEFOE, FORE, and BEFORE. Our results indicated that both FORE and BEFORE showed significant inhibition of tumor cell growth with cell viability $0\%10\%$ , demonstrating that pHSVtk/GCV served as an important agent to inhibit tumor cell proliferation (Fig. 5D). We also evaluated cancer cell inhibition in other lung cancer cell lines, such as H460 and A549, and found that BEFORE also showed apparent inhibition of these cells (Fig. S11). In contrast, pHSVtk showed much less toxicity to the normal cell line HPAEpiC, which indicated that treatment with BEFORE resulted in $74.5\pm7.9\%$ cell viability (Fig. S12).
We used CLSM and FACS analysis to validate the cell inhibition induced by different formulations. CLSM analysis indicated that using FORE or BEFORE caused significant red fluorescence (PIpositive, indicator of cell death) (Fig. 5E). In contrast, treatment with the formulations PBS, BEFO, BEFOR, or BEFOE showed nearly completely green fluorescence (calcein positive, indicator of live cells) (Fig. 5E). Consistent with the CLSM analysis, FACS analysis revealed that using FORE or BEFORE induced more than $54\%$ or $81\%$ apoptosis (Fig. 5F), which indicated that apoptosis is specifically induced by the delivery of GCV and pHSVtk together to the cells. With the assistance of BM, the biomimetic system directly enhanced the gene delivery in the cell with apoptosis, which may be due to the increase in cellular uptake of GCV/pHSVtkcontaining formulations. Based on the significant tumor cell inhibition induced by the biological bomb-loaded biomimetic nanoparticles, we consider that the bioinspired nanoparticles may have a remarkable antitumor effect in clinical practice.
## 3.5. Circulation lifetime and biodistribution in vivo
We explored the circulation lifetime and biodistribution of BEFORE in vivo. The blood retention of pHSVtk nearly attenuated to 0 within $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . However, BEFORE showed a significantly longer circulation lifetime, with blood retention of $25.1\pm3.9\%\mathrm{ID/g}$ within $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , which was 1/2 higher than that of the bare ZIF-8-based nanoparticle FORE (Fig. 6A). The evidence indicated that the cell membrane coating mimicked the surface properties of the source cells and improved the surface function of nanoparticles. The extension of the circulation lifetime implied the improvement of tumor accumulation [36]. Furthermore, we detected the distribution of nanoparticles using the in vivo tracking system. We found that BEFORE reached the tumor sites within $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ and was retained in the tumors for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ (Fig. 6B). However, pHSVtk or FORE showed much less fluorescence in the tumor sites (Fig. 6B), consistent with the ex vivo imaging of the extracted organs (Fig. 6C). The quantitative analysis of the fluorescence intensity indicated that organs such as the liver, spleen, lung, and kidney showed considerable values. At present, no delivery system can completely exclude nonspecific delivery. Furthermore, membrane proteins on BMSCs, such as CD47, may induce conjugation with SIRP- $\cdot\mathtt{\^a}$ on spleen tissues, which also occurs in red blood cell membrane-camouflaged nanoparticles [37]. One of the most attractive properties of the CEA promoter-encoded pHSVtk was its selectivity in lung cancer cells and low cytotoxicity in normal cells, which was beneficial for the development of precise cancer therapy. Thus, BEFORE improved the circulation lifetime and enhanced tumor accumulation, which significantly contributed to the effectiveness of lung cancer therapy.
## 3.6. Antitumor effect and biocompatibility evaluation in vivo
We evaluated the therapeutic effect of the BEFORE system in vivo using a subcutaneous model (NCI-H1299 cells). BEFORE showed efficient tumor inhibition, as the tumor volumes were approximately 1/20 of the saline-treated volumes (Fig. 7A&7B). FORE induced tumor inhibition with tumor volumes of approximately $1/4$ of the saline-treated volumes. However, other control groups, such as BEFO, BEFOR, or BEFOE, did not show significant tumor inhibition during the therapeutic period (Fig. 7A and 7B). During the treatment period, the body weight of the mice showed no significant changes (Fig. S13), implying that the formulations, including BEFORE, showed no apparent systemic toxicity. We further excised the tumor for HE analysis. There was significant tumor cell infiltration in the groups, such as saline, BEFO, BEFOR, and BEFOE. In contrast, BEFORE-treated mice showed similar tissue structures to normal mice (Fig. 7C). We also assessed apoptosis in the tumor tissues by TUNEL analysis. Compared to the salinetreated group, a large amount of tumor cell apoptosis was observed in the FORE-treated group, indicating that ZIF-8 could mediate both gene medicine and small molecule compounds for tumor inhibition. Notably, the biomimetic structure induced the most remarkable inhibition of tumor proliferation (Fig. 7D). Thus, the biomimetic delivery system has prominent capabilities for inhibiting tumor growth in lung therapy.
Biocompatibility is a critical factor paving the way for the potential biomedical applications of nanomaterials. We further evaluated the in vivo biosafety of the BEFORE system by hematoxylin-eosin (HE) staining of different organ sections. There were no distinguishable abnormalities of major organs throughout the observation periods (Fig. S14), which verified that this delivery system did not have adverse effects on the structures of the different organs. Compared with FORE, the BEFORE system showed a longer circulation lifetime and enhanced tumor accumulation, which implies that it may be a strong candidate for tumor treatment.
## 4. Conclusions
In this study, we engineered biomimetic ZIF-8 to deliver biological bombs with significant anticancer activity by inducing apoptosis in cancer cells. We are the first to use ZIF-8 to co-deliver suicide genes and small molecule compounds GCV effectively due to the high porosity and potential covalent bonds formed with the inorganic metal clusters in the MOF [33,34]. By modulating the surface coating, the biomimetic ZIF-8 nanovesicles showed high biosafety to normal cells, improved circulation lifetime, and specificity to inhibit the proliferation of lung cancer cells. The biomimetic delivery system enhanced the precision and reduced the dosage to $\sim$ one third compared with previous work [7,38]. Collectively, our study constructed a novel biomimetic metal–organic framework that was able to precisely navigate the biological bomb to the tumors and enhance the therapeutic effect on lung cancer. This strategy opens up a new avenue for lung cancer therapy, which requires further investigation for clinical applications.
